PRIVATE REFLECTION: {The deliberation strongly converges on the interpretation that Slc6a11 encodes a GABA transporter (GAT-3); knocking it down in sucrose-preferring rats reproduces the alcohol-preferring amygdala electrophysiology (−0.37 mV vs −0.38 mV, controls/sucrose around −0.16/−0.17 mV). This suggests alcohol-preferring rats have reduced GABA reuptake, higher extracellular GABA, more GABA receptor activation, and increased tonic inhibition in the amygdala. Consequently, GABA receptor agonists such as baclofen would be expected to reduce alcohol self-administration, whereas antagonists would not.

Most colleagues chose C or D; one chose B. C and D are nearly equivalent for the main mechanism, but C explicitly states higher extracellular GABA and specifies alcohol-preferring rats, though it has a probable typo: “compared to alcohol-preferring inhibition” instead of sucrose-preferring. D is cleaner on the comparison but omits higher extracellular GABA. Current leaning: C is likely the intended best answer, with D as the main alternative if the typo is treated as disqualifying. B/H/G are rejected because they claim GABA antagonists decrease alcohol self-administration. E is rejected because it says extracellular GABA is lower. F is rejected because it says sucrose-preferring rats have greater tonic inhibition.

Unresolved caveat: the sign of PS amplitude could be technically confusing—larger negative amplitude might ordinarily suggest greater neuronal spiking—but the exam’s intended logic clearly links Slc6a11 knockdown to increased GABAergic tonic inhibition. Keep this caveat in mind if later discussion challenges C/D.}

